hi there  it's uh 10 o'clock at night here i have  to admit that i'm a little tired  and um i'm gonna talk a little bit about  you know accumulating words  and the idea that um  you know it's not language learning is  not a sprint  and so i've never been that concerned  about how quickly i can learn something  unless for example  when i went to romania i had to  try to learn up romanian in two months  in order to go there and be able to  communicate but otherwise  i'm quite content to labor away  gradually accumulating words and  gradually accumulating a sense of the  language knowing that every little bit i  do even though i'm not aware of any  improvement  it is  accumulating it is  getting me closer to my eventual goal  and obviously the first 500 000 words of  the language you got to learn them and  you learn them typically in beginner  type texts that are very simple but  beyond that it really to my mind it  doesn't matter whether i learn the  word 1500 and the frequency list or the  word 5000 on the frequency list it  doesn't much matter to me because  eventually i'm going to have to learn  them all because eventually i want to  have a large vocabulary  so but i have to admit that it's easier  on the one hand it's easier if you have  texts with only 10 or 15 new words on  the other hand you're acquiring fewer  new words so  uh you know when i was learning russian  i didn't mind when i first i i got out  of the beginner text into tolstoy the  kreutzer sonata 50 new words i slugged  away it didn't bother me most people  perhaps don't have that amount of  patience  so right now i've got this uh podcast of  kim jung has  which in retrospect is probably too  difficult and a little too esoteric for  me a little too literary  uh not necessarily the vocabulary that  i'm looking for that i would want to use  there's one here he's talking about a  musician and treble and bass uh you know  and then he's talking about some medical  condition and a whole bunch of stuff  that's a little bit not so useful but i  don't mind  uh  and of course the fact is that i i like  listening to him  and i've paid now to have these uh  podcasts transcribed and i've got about  20 of them transcribed and we have them  in the uh  in our link library and i enjoy them and  all  but there's a lot of vocabulary that i  probably won't use right away  however still 70 80 of the vocabulary is  as as in all content as we know it  consists of the most common words so i'm  still gaining that sense of rhythm of  the language and so forth  now you have to kind of notice your  minor victories and a victory today as  i'm going through  saving now  so a sign that you're getting somewhere  is when you start to see  more and more and more and more words  that you know and fewer and fewer words  that you don't know  and this is quite apparent when you're  working at link because my normal op  you know method of operation is to go  through a text  looking up the words i don't know the  blue words linking them and these  podcasts of kim jong-un are between 30  and 50 minutes long so these are these  are not short lessons  and  as i'm going through them now  i find that as i you know because i can  just by hitting my  key here my entry key i go to the next  word the next word the next word and  more and more of the blue words i  already know them i don't need to look  them up they're variations of words that  i know that's why they're blue because  they're a different form of a word that  i've already learned  and so i'm now able to go through sort  of a 30 40 minute podcast the text of a  podcast saving words  uh in you know 15 20 minutes whereas it  used to take like an hour and a half  and when i first started linking korean  words i i had no  sense of whether the meaning was even  close to what i was looking for whereas  now the whole process has become easier  faster i feel i'm in control  and so while i'm not speaking so  wonderfully fluently  and i still have trouble when i try to  listen to the podcast to understand it  i know i'm progressing i know that i'm  chipping away at the goal and you know i  don't think it's unrealistic in in any  language to like my goal is to know 20  30 000 words that's where i want to get  to so i'm not fussed about whether it's  word 4000 in the frequency list or six  thousand in the frequency list  eventually i gotta learn twenty thousand  words and so i just labor away  hopefully with content that has some  appeal at some level and kim jong's  content does appeal in terms of the kind  of material that he's dealing with most  of the time reference to this novel to  some  american author or to some french author  and he talks about when he visited in  vancouver and and all of this stuff is  kind of intriguing to me even though  when he talks i don't understand it that  well but when i read through it i can  but the point i guess i want to make is  that uh even though i'm tired  beaten up here fighting my way through  these difficult texts i know that i'm  chipping away at this goal of eventually  having this large vocabulary in korean  uh i've only got 11 days to go  i don't think that i'll be speaking  appreciably better 10 or 11 days from  now than i speak today i am going to try  to line up  uh some tutor to speak to so that i will  have like an exit  conversation in korean and i'm gonna  promise to put up some subtitles just to  see where i end up  but i think the big achievement is that  i will have tremendously increased my  passive vocabulary and so that over time  these are sort of investments  in the language that will come back to  me as  the rest of my language skills catch up  so there you have it that's where we are  on day 79 bye for now

